--QUES14:How many Clustered Indexes can you have? 
/*A table can have only one clustered index because the data can be physically sorted in only one order. Additional indexes must be non-clustered.*/

--QUES15:cluster and non clustered index 
/*A clustered index stores the data rows physically in order, so range queries are faster, and the leaf level contains the actual data. 
A non-clustered index stores only key values and pointers to the data rows, allowing multiple non-clustered indexes per table. 
Non-clustered indexes are suitable for lookup queries, while clustered indexes are ideal for ordered access or range scans.*/

--QUES16:Questions on Indexes, showed a query and indexes on the tables in the query and asked how to improve query performance by modifying indexes. (There are around 10 questions of similar type) 
/*When a query is slow, I first check the execution plan to see missing or unused indexes. I might add a new index, create a covering index, include 
additional columns, or add a filtered index. I also consider changing column order in the index keys or dropping unnecessary indexes to reduce maintenance overhead.*/

--QUES17:Difference between clustered and non-clustered indexes? 
/*A clustered index determines the physical order of the data and stores the actual rows at the leaf level, allowing only one per table. A non-clustered index stores a separate structure with pointers to the rows, allowing multiple indexes per table. Clustered indexes are better for range queries, and non-clustered indexes are better for specific lookups.*/

--QUES18:How do you create clustered index, told to create clustered index on given result set? 
/*You cannot directly create a clustered index on a result set. Instead, you need to insert the result into a table and then create a clustered index on that table, 
specifying the column(s) that determine the physical sort order of the data.*/

